{% comment %}
  The board: three columns, each holding one or two cards.

  Slots map to the grid in fixed positions — 1/2 left, 3/4 middle, 5/6 right —
  so a station stays where the user put it. A column with one active slot gives
  that card the full height; with two, they split it with a rule between.

  Rows are trimmed by the framework's Content Limiter rather than by the
  backend: `data-content-limiter` on a `.content` element measures the rendered
  height in the browser and drops what does not fit. The backend therefore
  sends more rows than any card can show (16) and the panel decides, so a card
  alone in its column fills the space without the server knowing the layout.
  The explicit `data-content-max-height` is what makes the cut land between
  rows instead of through one.

  Row shape, matching the dashboard it replaces:
    [badge] [destination, clipped] [time, right-aligned]

  Rows are data tuples, not objects, to stay inside TRMNL's 5KB payload cap:
    r[0] badge   r[1] destination   r[2] time   r[3] 1 when urgent
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
  Heights the Content Limiter trims to, measured against the rendered panel.

  Scaled by device: a TRMNL X is 1404px tall against the OG's 480, so a budget
  tuned for one leaves most of the other empty. `trmnl.device.height` is what
  the platform reports, so the budget follows the hardware rather than being
  guessed per model.

  The ratios come from the OG measurement — 374 of 480 usable for a full card,
  163 for one sharing a column — applied to whatever height the device has.
  A percentage point either way decides whether the last row lands whole or
  leaves a fragment, so these were tuned against the render rather than
  reasoned about.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign device_height = trmnl.device.height | default: 480 %}
{% assign full_height = device_height | times: 72 | divided_by: 100 %}
{% assign split_height = device_height | times: 34 | divided_by: 100 %}

{% comment %}
  Two things the first renders exposed.

  **A badge must not wrap.** A railroad branch name runs onto a second line
  otherwise, doubling the row height and pushing the card over its budget. A
  one-line clamp keeps every row identical.

  **A badge must not be squeezed either.** The clamp alone let a long
  destination crush the badge — the bus card rendered a four-character "Q101"
  as "Q1…" while fifteen-character railroad badges beside it were fine, because
  "STEINWAY via STEINWAY ST" was taking the width.

  The fix is simply to leave the badge alone: no clamp, no width. `data-clamp`
  was what broke it — the clamp engine measures a badge the flex row has
  already squeezed and truncates to fit that squeezed box, so a long
  destination two elements away decided how much of "Q101" survived.

  Without it the badge sizes to its own text and the destination gives way
  instead, which is the right priority: the route is what a rider scans for.
  A fixed width was also tried and is wrong in both directions — far too wide
  for a one-character subway bullet, too narrow for "Port Washington".

  **The time is set at `title--small`, not `value--*`.** The smallest value
  size is 20px and took about 55px of a 90px direction column — well over half
  the width for a clock. At 16px it matches the destination beside it and
  leaves room for the destination to be shown at all.

  **`.content` caps itself at a prose-reading width.** The framework sets
  `max-width: var(--richtext-content-max-width)` on it, which is right for
  paragraphs and wrong for a departure column: it left a full-width card using
  169px of its 253, and squeezed the subway columns to 87px. `w--full`
  overrides it.
{% endcomment %}

<div class="view">
  <div class="layout">
    <div class="grid grid--cols-3 h--full">

      {% comment %}
        Left column takes slots 1 and 2, middle 3 and 4, right 5 and 6.

        Compared as numbers rather than by splitting a string: the backend
        sends `s` as a number, and a split string yields strings, so `1 == "1"`
        is false in Liquid and the whole board renders blank.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% for column_index in (0..2) %}
        {% assign top_slot = column_index | times: 2 | plus: 1 %}
        {% assign bottom_slot = top_slot | plus: 1 %}

        {% comment %}
          How many of this column's two slots the user configured. A slot left
          Off never reaches the board, so a column may hold two cards, one, or
          none — and a lone card takes the column's whole height.
        {% endcomment %}
        {% assign card_count = 0 %}
        {% for card in c %}
          {% if card.s == top_slot or card.s == bottom_slot %}
            {% assign card_count = card_count | plus: 1 %}
          {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}

        <div class="col col--start gap--small">
          {% for card in c %}
            {% if card.s == top_slot or card.s == bottom_slot %}

                {% comment %} A rule between two cards sharing a column. {% endcomment %}
                {% if card.s == bottom_slot and card_count > 1 %}
                  <div class="border--h-black w--full"></div>
                {% endif %}

                {% comment %}
                  Card heading. Subway cards carry route bullets; the others
                  are named by their station alone.
                {% endcomment %}
                {% comment %}
                  Station name first, bullets after, and at most four of them.

                  Leading with the bullets crushed "Times Sq-42 St" to "Ti..."
                  — the name is what identifies a card, so it takes the space.
                  And a twelve-route complex wrapped its bullets onto a second
                  line, making one heading taller than the rest; past about
                  four the bullets stop telling a rider anything the name has
                  not, so the rest are dropped.
                {% endcomment %}
                <div class="flex flex--row items--center gap--small">
                  <span class="title title--small md:title--base lg:title--large flex--1" data-clamp="1">{{ card.n }}</span>
                  {% if card.b %}
                    {% for route in card.b limit: 4 %}
                      <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--filled">{{ route }}</span>
                    {% endfor %}
                  {% endif %}
                </div>
                <div class="border--h-40 w--full"></div>

                {% if card.e %}
                  {% comment %}
                    A failed fetch, reported in place. Distinct from a card
                    with nothing due — that is a fact about the service, not a
                    fault, and says so below instead.
                  {% endcomment %}
                  <span class="label label--small label--outline">Unavailable</span>
                  <span class="description" data-clamp="2">{{ card.e }}</span>

                {% else %}
                  {% if card_count == 1 %}
                    {% assign card_height = full_height %}
                  {% else %}
                    {% assign card_height = split_height %}
                  {% endif %}

                  {% comment %}
                    A card's columns sit **side by side**, not stacked.

                    A subway card has two — one per direction — and stacking
                    them put the second inside the same height budget as the
                    first, so the limiter cut it off entirely and the card
                    looked like a terminal that only ran one way. Bus cards
                    have one column per stop ID, which is one direction each,
                    so the same rule applies to them.

                    Each gets `flex--1` so they divide the card evenly
                    whatever they hold, and its own limiter so a busy
                    direction cannot crowd out a quiet one. The row carries
                    `flex--top`, without which the shorter column centres
                    itself vertically and the two headings no longer line up.
                  {% endcomment %}
                  <div class="flex flex--row gap--small w--full flex--top">
                    {% comment %}
                      When every departure in a column goes to the same place,
                      the destination repeats down the column and tells the
                      rider nothing — every northbound 7 at Vernon Blvd ends
                      at Flushing-Main St. Dropping it matters here because
                      two side-by-side columns leave about 40px for a
                      destination, which is unreadable, while the badge and
                      time are what a rider actually needs.
                    {% endcomment %}
                    {% for col in card.k %}
                    {% assign distinct = "" %}
                    {% for r in col.r %}
                      {% unless distinct contains r[1] %}
                        {% assign distinct = distinct | append: r[1] | append: "|" %}
                      {% endunless %}
                    {% endfor %}
                    {% assign distinct_count = distinct | split: "|" | size %}
                    {% comment %}
                      Drop the destination only when it repeats every row *and*
                      the card is split into two narrow columns. Every
                      northbound 7 at Vernon Blvd ends at Flushing-Main St, so
                      there the label says nothing and the width is better
                      spent. A single full-width column keeps it even when it
                      repeats — on a bus card the destination is the only thing
                      distinguishing two routes at the same stop.
                    {% endcomment %}
                    {% if card.k.size > 1 and distinct_count < 2 %}
                      {% assign hide_destination = true %}
                    {% else %}
                      {% assign hide_destination = false %}
                    {% endif %}
                    <div class="flex--1 content w--full"
                         data-content-limiter="true"
                         data-content-max-height="{{ card_height }}">
                      {% if col.h != "" %}
                        <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--underline">{{ col.h }}</span>
                      {% endif %}

                      {% for r in col.r %}
                        {% comment %}
                          Suppress a destination that merely repeats its badge.
                          A branch's trains usually end at the branch's own
                          namesake, so "Hempstead | Hempstead" is the common
                          case at a railroad terminal — it costs the width the
                          time needs and tells the rider nothing.
                        {% endcomment %}
                        {% comment %}
                          Suppress a destination that repeats its badge, and
                          shorten one that merely extends it — a "New Haven"
                          badge beside "New Haven-State St" says the branch
                          twice, and the extra length squeezes the badge into
                          wrapping, which makes that one row taller than its
                          neighbours.
                        {% endcomment %}
                        {% assign destination = r[1] %}
                        {% if hide_destination %}
                          {% assign destination = "" %}
                        {% elsif destination == r[0] %}
                          {% assign destination = "" %}
                        {% else %}
                          {% assign badge_prefix = r[0] | append: "-" %}
                          {% if destination contains badge_prefix %}
                            {% assign destination = destination | remove_first: badge_prefix %}
                          {% endif %}
                        {% endif %}
                        {% if r[3] == 1 %}
                          {% comment %}
                            Urgent: departures you must leave for now. Outlined
                            rather than inverted — at a ten-minute threshold
                            with a typical walk most rows qualify, and a panel
                            of solid black rows has no emphasis left.
                          {% endcomment %}
                          <div class="flex flex--row flex--between items--center gap--small b--black bw--1">
                            <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--outline">{{ r[0] }}</span>
                            {% if destination == blank %}
                              <span class="flex--1"></span>
                            {% else %}
                              <span class="title title--small md:title--base flex--1" data-clamp="1">{{ destination }}</span>
                            {% endif %}
                            <span class="title title--small md:title--base">{{ r[2] }}</span>
                          </div>
                        {% else %}
                          <div class="flex flex--row flex--between items--center gap--small">
                            <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--filled">{{ r[0] }}</span>
                            {% if destination == blank %}
                              <span class="flex--1"></span>
                            {% else %}
                              <span class="title title--small md:title--base flex--1" data-clamp="1">{{ destination }}</span>
                            {% endif %}
                            <span class="title title--small md:title--base">{{ r[2] }}</span>
                          </div>
                        {% endif %}
                      {% endfor %}

                      {% assign row_count = col.r | size %}
                      {% if row_count == 0 %}
                        {% comment %}
                          Empty is not an error: the feed answered and had
                          nothing due. Saying so is the whole point — the app
                          does not guess whether service ended, the stop is
                          peak-only, or everything is inside the walk time.
                        {% endcomment %}
                        <span class="description">No upcoming departures</span>
                      {% endif %}
                    </div>
                    {% endfor %}
                  </div>
                {% endif %}

            {% endif %}
          {% endfor %}
        </div>

      {% endfor %}

    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="title_bar">
    <span class="title">NYC Transit</span>
    {% if d %}
      {% comment %}
        The backend substitutes a demo board when nothing is configured, so
        the panel must say whose stations these are. Without it a new
        installer sees Times Square and reasonably assumes their own form
        took effect.
      {% endcomment %}
      <span class="instance">Example - choose your stations in settings</span>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</div>
